   429	### 4.2 STAR-ABC: what the star really is
   430	
   431	> **THEOREM STAR-ABC (PROVED-HERE).** Let `P, Q` be nonconstant with `deg Q = b_1 = dV_2`,
   432	> `deg P = a_1 = eV_2`, `ρ ≠ 0`. The following are equivalent:
   433	> ```text
   434	>   (1)  d Q P' − e P Q'  =  κ  ∈ C^* ;
   435	>   (2)  deg( Q^e − ρ P^d )  =  V_2(de − d − e) + 1   for some ρ ≠ 0, with P, Q and
   436	>        C := Q^e − ρP^d pairwise coprime and squarefree ;
   437	>   (3)  R := Q^e/P^d : P^1 → P^1 has degree deV_2 and is branched over exactly three
   438	>        points, with profiles  [e^{dV_2}] over 0,  [d^{eV_2}] over ∞, and
   439	>        [(d+e)V_2 − 1, 1^{V_2(de−d−e)+1}] over R(∞).
   440	> ```
   441	> `V_2(de−d−e)+1` is the **Mason–Stothers/ABC bound**, so (2) says the bottom star is an
   442	> **ABC-extremal (Davenport–Stothers) pair**, and (3) says it is a **dessin d'enfant**.
   443	
   444	*Proof.* (1) ⇒ (2): with `C = Q^e − ρP^d`, differentiating `Q^e/P^d = ρ + C/P^d` gives
   445	`C'P − dCP' = −Q^{e−1}·(dQP'−ePQ') = −κ Q^{e−1}`. Comparing degrees, the leading
   446	coefficient of `C'P − dCP'` is `c_γ(γ − deV_2)` with `γ = deg C`, hence
   447	`γ + a_1 − 1 = deg Q^{e−1} = b_1(e−1)` and `γ = V_2(de−d−e)+1`; a multiple root of `C`
   448	would force `Q = 0` there, hence `P = 0`, contradicting `gcd(P,Q) = 1` (STAR-SIMPLE).
   449	(2) ⇒ (1): Mason–Stothers applied to `A = Q^e`, `B = −ρP^d`, `C = A+B`. The standard
   450	proof shows `(ABC)/rad(ABC) | W(A,B) = AB'−A'B`, and here
   451	`W(A,B) = −ρ Q^{e−1}P^{d−1}(dQP'−ePQ')` while `(ABC)/rad(ABC) = ρ Q^{e−1}P^{d−1}`
   452	(the three radicals being `Q`, `P`, `C`); equality of degrees in the ABC bound forces
   453	`dQP' − ePQ'` to be a constant, nonzero because `A/B` is nonconstant.
   454	(2) ⇔ (3): `R^{-1}(0)` is the `b_1` simple roots of `Q` each with multiplicity `e`,
   455	`R^{-1}(∞)` the `a_1` simple roots of `P` each with multiplicity `d` (total `deV_2` each,
   456	so `π = ∞` is neither a zero nor a pole), and `R' = −eκQ^{e−1}/P^{d+1}` vanishes nowhere
